  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 124 PM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High 1034 mb pressure centered over the Northeast this \nafternoon will shift southeastward over to the mid Atlantic coast, \ndrifting offshore over the next few days. This will turn flow more \nsoutherly to southwesterly across northern New York and Vermont, \nresulting in above average temperatures this week. Highs Monday and \nTuesday afternoons will be widespread 60s. Lows fall only into the \n40s tonight and Monday night. A tilted mid to upper level ridge will \nbe positioned to our west through about Tuesday, moving eastwards \nthis week. Depending on the positioning of an upper level vort max \nand surface boundaries, there could be just enough forcing and lift \nto produce some clouds and light rain, mainly along the \ninternational border and on high terrain on Tuesday and Tuesday \nnight. The odd rain shower is possible Monday night in the Northeast \nKingdom, but generally best convergence is north of the border at \nthis time. Deterministic model solutions continue to vary widely on \nthe position of these features, so uncertainty remains relatively \nhigh, even on Tuesday and Tuesday night. Highest chance of some \nmeasurable precipitation looks to be Tuesday afternoon and evening \nwith 35-55% chance of rain for about the northern half of the \nforecast area, lower chances south. Regardless, temperatures \ncontinue to look warm for this time of year heading into mid and \nlate week with highs likely reaching the upper 60s and lower 70s by \nWednesday afternoon as southerly to southwesterly winds advect in \neven more warmth and 925 mb level temps climb to 11-16 C. Lows are \nexpected to fall into the mid 40s to mid 50s Tuesday and Wednesday \nnights.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Confidence continues to increase that a relatively \nstrong low pressure and associated cold front will sweep through \nnorthern New York and Vermont by the end of the week. A closed low \nwill be over the Upper Great Lakes by Wednesday. While timing is \nstill a bit uncertain, we should see channeled flow keeping \nwinds over Lake Champlain gusting to near 30mph on Thursday with\nrain overtaking the CWA on Friday ahead of the low. As the cold\nfront sweeps through, we'll see high temperatures drop from the\nupper 60s Thursday down to the upper 40s by Saturday. A number \nof model solutions have the system interacting with another low \noff the Atlantic Seaboard, causing the low to partially cutoff \nfrom the main flow and allowing a slight chance of rain to \npersist through next Sunday.",
